# Persistence

## Naming Conventions

- Name types `*Repository`, `*RepositoryImpl`, `*SqlConfigurationProperties`, `*SqlColumns`, `*Model`.
- Name SQL keys `sql.<resource>.<operation>` (e.g. `sql.holidays.find-by-id`).

## Rules

- Use `JdbcClient` for relational persistence.
- Use `XyzRepository` + `XyzRepositoryImpl` when the module has multiple adapters or non-trivial domain logic; otherwise one `@Repository` class.
- Declare column names in a feature-scoped `*SqlColumns` class; use those constants as named parameter keys.
- Return `Optional<T>` from single-resource lookups.
- Parameterize all SQL. Externalize statements into one `@PropertySource` XML file per feature.
- Declare `@PropertySource` on the `@Configuration` class that owns the `JdbcClient` bean.
- Access SQL through a `@Validated` `*SqlConfigurationProperties` record; annotate each SQL field with `@NotBlank`.
- Keep models free of persistence annotations.

## Good vs bad

```java
// GOOD
@Repository
class HolidayRepositoryImpl implements HolidayRepository {
  private final JdbcClient jdbcClient;
  private final HolidaySqlConfigurationProperties sql;

  HolidayRepositoryImpl(JdbcClient jdbcClient, HolidaySqlConfigurationProperties sql) {
    this.jdbcClient = Objects.requireNonNull(jdbcClient, "jdbcClient");
    this.sql = Objects.requireNonNull(sql, "sql");
  }

  @Override
  public Optional<HolidayModel> findById(long id) {
    return jdbcClient.sql(sql.findById())
        .param(HolidaySqlColumns.ID, id)
        .query(this::mapRow)
        .optional();
  }
}

// BAD — ORM and inline SQL
public interface HolidayRepository extends JpaRepository<HolidayEntity, Long> {}

@Entity
public class HolidayEntity { @Id Long id; }

jdbcClient.sql("SELECT * FROM holidays WHERE id = %s".formatted(id));
```

```xml
<!-- GOOD — src/main/resources/sql/holiday-sql.xml -->
<properties>
  <entry key="sql.holidays.find-by-id">SELECT id, name FROM holidays WHERE id = :id</entry>
</properties>
```

## Safety Guards

- Never build SQL with `String#formatted`, concatenation, or request-derived replacement.
- Never use JPA, Hibernate, `@Entity`, or `JpaRepository`.
